(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pulm-anaphylaxis-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Anaphylaxis Treatment Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which description defines anaphylaxis?","choices":["A severe, systemic allergic reaction","Widespread reversible airway narrowing","Inflammation of one or more bronchi","An allergic reaction of the skin alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anaphylaxis is a severe, systemic allergic reaction with multisystem involvement: skin, airway, vascular system and gastrointestinal tract. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which corticosteroid listed for anaphylaxis can be given intramuscularly or intravenously?","choices":["Prednisone","Methylprednisolone","Hydroxyzine","Famotidine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Methylprednisolone is the corticosteroid listed for intramuscular or intravenous use in anaphylaxis; prednisone is the other agent. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"By which route is epinephrine generally preferred in anaphylaxis when the patient is not coding?","choices":["Intravenous","Subcutaneous","Intramuscular","Inhaled"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine can be given intramuscularly, subcutaneously or intravenously, and the intramuscular route is generally preferred unless the patient is coding. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which drug used in anaphylaxis is a histamine H1 blocker?","choices":["Famotidine","Diphenhydramine","Albuterol","Prednisone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diphenhydramine and hydroxyzine are the histamine H1 blockers; they relieve rash, pruritus and hives. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How do corticosteroids act in anaphylaxis?","choices":["They block histamine at its receptors","They stimulate alpha and beta receptors","They expand the intravascular volume","They block inflammatory mediator production"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids block production of inflammatory mediators at the cellular level and work on all aspects of anaphylaxis, though they take several hours. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which complication can an epinephrine overdose cause?","choices":["Cerebrovascular hemorrhage","Adrenal suppression","Peripheral neuritis","Oral candidiasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine overdose can lead to cerebrovascular hemorrhage, myocardial infarction and pulmonary edema. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which effect of epinephrine comes from its alpha receptor activity?","choices":["Bronchodilation","Less gastric acid","Less inflammation","Vasoconstriction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine's alpha effects are vasoconstriction and decreased vascular permeability; its beta effect is bronchial smooth muscle relaxation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is listed for albuterol given in anaphylaxis?","choices":["Bradycardia","Diaphoresis","Tremor","Oral thrush"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Albuterol's listed adverse effects are anxiousness, tremor and tachycardia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which kind of agent is epinephrine?","choices":["A synthetic beta-2 agonist","A systemic corticosteroid","A histamine H1 blocker","An endogenous catecholamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine (adrenalin) is an endogenous catecholamine that acts on alpha and beta receptors. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 51"}]};
